From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: fly2xj <fly2xj@msn.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 13:36:56 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] Switch 2 controllers: a low-latency link and a queue of their
 own

The controllers send one input report a Bluetooth LE connection event, so macOS's default 30 ms connection interval held them to about 33 reports a second. Connect with kCBConnectOptionRequiresLowLatency, which gets the 15 ms interval, about 66 reports a second, as WaveBird (github.com/murphyjt/wavebird) does for the same controllers.

Run the transport (the central's delegate, timeouts, the LED keepalive, the rumble tick) on a serial queue of its own at user-interactive priority, so a busy main thread neither delays nor bunches the reports. The front end's callbacks still run on the main queue, each with a copy of the pad's state; read() is coalesced to one pending call a pad, with buttons pressed meanwhile kept, so a stalled main thread gets no backlog.

The clock no longer assumes 30 ms: s2_clock_sample learns the interval from the controller clock's steps, so a lost report is counted against the link's own interval, and the second of rest that sets the gyro offset is as many reports as the link's rate makes.

Not checked on hardware: the interval the controllers get with the key (DM_SW2_VERBOSE=1 logs it), and rumble and motion with the new queue.

Upstream-Status: forwarded seedds/nxnative#2422
Reason: Switch 2 controllers report at about 66 Hz instead of 33, on a queue of their own
Issue: LNPANDY/NX2Apple#1202

